The collection hierarchy.

Almost every program juggles groups of things — a list of orders, a set of tags, a map from user id to user. Java's collections framework is the standard library's answer: a small set of interfaces that describe kinds of groupings, and a handful of implementations behind them. Learn the shape of the hierarchy once, and the whole framework becomes predictable.

The key split is between interface and implementation, and the framework is deliberately two layers. The interfaces name a capability — list, set, queue, map. They say what you can do: ordered and indexed, no duplicates, key-to-value. The implementations are the concrete classes — array-list, hash-set, hash-map, array-deque. They decide how: the actual data structure, and its performance. And the habit, straight from module two's "program to interfaces", is to type your variables by the interface, but pick the implementation with new. So you write list of string equals new array-list. You think of it as "a list", you just happen to use an array-list underneath. Now callers depend on list, not array-list, and you can swap the implementation later without touching them.

Here's the map of the framework. At the root is Iterable — anything you can walk with a for-each loop. Below it sits Collection, the parent of the three types that hold elements. A list is an ordered, indexed sequence, and duplicates are allowed — you can ask for "the third element", and a list can hold the same value twice. A set is a group with no duplicates; it's about membership, not position. And a queue, or a deque, arranges elements for ordered processing — first-in-first-out, or from both ends.

Map stands slightly apart. It holds key-to-value pairs, not single elements, so it is not a collection — you can't just for-each over a map directly. It's still part of the framework, and its keys and its values are themselves collection-like.

One more thing that's true everywhere: every collection is generic, parameterised by the type it holds — a list of string, a map from long to user, a set of tag. That angle-bracket part is the element type, and it's checked by the compiler, so a list of string can never accidentally hold an integer, and you never cast on the way out. Generics get their own module, module five; here, we just use them. And the diamond — the empty angle brackets on the right — lets the compiler infer the type argument, so you write new array-list with empty brackets.

So here's where this module goes. We'll take the big three in turn — list, set, and map — then queue and deque, and then the cross-cutting skills that apply to all of them: iterating safely, building immutable collections and defending mutable ones, sorting with comparable and comparator, and the equals and hash-code contracts that hash-based and tree-based collections quietly depend on. We close by choosing the right collection for a problem. The framework is large, but it's really the same few ideas repeated — pick the interface for the job, and pick the implementation for the performance.
